so you started out in a different  environment in public radio  uh and so how was that experience for  you i guess that was relatively early in  your career  yeah i started i you know right after  college i  had a crisis of identity and i didn't  know what i was going to do so i started  all first in  and working as a financial advisor for  for morgan stanley dean witter and um it  was actually kind of during that  miserable commute around the dc  beltway i discovered public radio and  over  a number of years i kind of set my  sights on public radio because i  realized that's really what i  wanted to be doing i just loved the  craft of it and i loved  the way that they told stories i'd never  had a desire to go into tv  and i also didn't really do internships  through college because i was you know  working full-time and just  a little bit lost in general so it  wasn't until after a while after college  i discovered npr  and set my sights and i started at npr  in 2002 and um  loved every day from from the you know  first minute i was there  so so you were attracted to npr and i  have to say when i was out there  campaigning around the country npr was  like its own  oasis of news like there were folks that  listened to it  the local npr stations  uh seemed very free they were very pure  you'd go in  and they had listeners who really wanted  a particular  version of both the news and the  listening experience  uh you were attracted to that and it  sounds like you were there from 2002 to  2013  more or less i mean you were doing some  other stuff but it was all  uh public radio directed is that right  it's like 11 years or so  yes that's correct yeah um and  yeah i mean i just this morning i was  listening i listened  to kqed frequently which is a san  francisco station and you know it's  it's such a you know pleasure because  the you know the local  um public affairs program forum they  were they covered  um the local um uh drug overdose  epidemic and how it's gotten worse  through the pandemic and then they moved  on to  um the the new coveted vaccines for  another 30 minutes and it's this long  in-depth conversation and things that i  didn't even know i needed to know but  yeah i mean it's it's it's just so much  more and so  such better information that you can get  yeah and npr is supported  through um public funding um though  though npr now has  so many listeners and sponsors and  advertisers that  it's largely self-sustaining and a lot  of people have also donated to it  um so it's like a combination um  and then you wound up at msnbc which  uh it seemed like your  experience was a bit different uh how  was the experience  i kind of came into it with the attitude  i mean we knew that  everything was kind of you know we knew  that rating i knew that ratings  um played a part in our daily lives  i didn't understand the extent of it and  i thought  coming in i could bring my skills that i  had  in public radio and part of that is  really trying to find new and  interesting and compelling  stories and angles and voices and they  would  want to you know they would be they  would welcome that because  in my mind i felt like one of my big  criticisms of  cable news coming into it was the  repetition it was just i just felt like  it was  you know you're watching the same five  or six stories kind of all day  throughout the day so um i thought that  that was an opportunity for me  to come in and you know make things  better  right um so i i struggled with that for  a  few years um i really i you know i tried  different types of stories and kind of i  strengthened my pitches and  realized okay they might not listen to  me the first time but i'll keep coming  back and you know trying to  to sell this other idea or strengthen my  argument maybe they don't  you know maybe it's not intuitive why  something is a good idea  and over time i you know i i  got a little bit frustrated but um then  started to get  at the same time a little bit of  seniority so i was more involved in the  planning meetings you know with the  senior producer  and the executive producer and or the  senior producers  there was usually more than one and at  that point i  realized the extent that they really  that the ratings really drove all of the  decisions  and it was it was it's down to you know  it's the first thing out of their mouths  as soon as you throw out an idea and  they don't necessarily talk that way  in the wider editorial meetings with the  full staff  it really was in this the smaller  planning meetings  you know it's the first thing out of  their mouths like how things rated the  night before how  they thought something did and those  those ratings are broken down by quarter  hours so they have a pretty good  understanding of what did well and what  didn't do well  and so they will track the topics of  course  and they'll track the guests you know so  if you know if the guest does well  or if regress guest rates well or not  they really  rely on that for  almost every decision so um it  that's it wasn't until the last few  years  when the reality um became  you know pretty stark to me and then you  know i wasn't gonna be able to fight  that fight  you know there wasn't you know these  people are just doing their jobs  and it's all out of their hands it's out  of lawrence's hands it's out of phil  griffin's hands  it's it's at a higher way higher pay  level um  where that where the problems are you  know one of the things you said was that  look these are good people  uh you know trying to do good work but  uh they're put in a position where their  job does not necessarily produce the  kind of journalism you'd want it to  which is something i completely get  and empathize with is that if you put  people in a position where their  incentives are all steering them one  direction you kind of expect them to  end up uh listening to those incentives  you know it's like  and if you don't listen to those  incentives and let's say your ratings  underperform  uh consistently then someone's going to  make a change and so like that's the  the race you're always running uh  like were there other people that ever  expressed any misgiving maybe someone  else from like a similar background as  you  yes um those conversations didn't happen  very often but  over the last year or two when i was  there there were times when i would have  conversations in private with other  producers  and um certainly they  you know with a couple of people um  they said i think we're making things  worse  you know um some seem to think that  yeah what we're doing here is probably  benefiting trump and would help him get  elected again  the person who said we are a cancer that  is that's one of those conversations it  was somebody  in the building um uh  and has been in the the business a long  time so  um but it wasn't something that people  talked about in the open  you know it wasn't until i went in and  sat down like kind of one-on-one with  them  um otherwise that the the notion that  ratings drive everything it's just  it's really built into the process and  it's it's  of course you know that's just that's  just what you do and  you know most of those people came up  through  the commercial tv system somehow and i  obviously have a public radio background  and  none of the editorial decisions i ever  made before were based on  how it would rate or how big of an  audience we thought we would have i mean  we always wanted a big audience but you  know you hope that you know what you're  doing is compelling enough to hold  people's attention um but that's not  what  determines those decisions and so for me  you know it was like it really raked  against my  nerves every time i heard them say  something like you know that's not going  to rain  when it's something that i knew would be  a good topic some of the decisions were  made out of ideological  reasons um but it also  kind of drives the division that that  we're seeing and the polarization in  this  in the country so they might be pumping  up you know  um ideas that are exciting to some  people but then there was never you know  they don't get excited about the  you know someone with a more nuanced or  moderate take on things  and um so that that just kind of keeps  driving  everybody apart and there's you know you  know there's no  sense that okay there is some benefit to  compromise  every now and then i just i know that  that's not a popular idea right now  because we've gotten so far away from  that but  um you know the the in my sense is  that it just when you  stand back and look at the totality of  you know all the different ways that the  financial incentives affect  the content it's just driving us down  the wrong path and it's only getting  worse  the way that i see it you drew a couple  of examples  in your resignation letter which i  recommend that everyone  find and read because i just thought it  was so brave and  incisive and true  so thank you for it and i'm a huge  admirer of yours you did something  out of principle that the vast vast  majority of people  don't ever do and if enough people did  what you did we might actually have a  chance  and i don't mean just in media though  the media is a significant part of this  but  you know in in many different  environments where your financial  incentives are  to uh just pretend it's okay  thank you for listening in i hope you  enjoyed this conversation  if you did please do subscribe to yang  speaks and click on notifications so we  can let you know  every time we have a new episode

